  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Niger - Floods</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operation targets:

250,000 people, focusing on vulnerable groups such as women, children, the elderly, and people with chronic illnesses or disabilities.
Households most affected by floods, especially those in remote or underserved areas.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operational strategy addresses the 2024 catastrophic floods in Niger, which have caused:

Over 1 million people affected.
322 deaths and significant injuries.
Destruction of 106,700 houses, 19,274 livestock, and 13,917 hectares of crops.
Emergence of a cholera outbreak, with 479 cases and 13 deaths reported.
Severe damage to infrastructure, livelihoods, and public health systems.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The response focuses on:

Emergency Relief:
Distribution of shelter kits, food, and cash assistance to meet immediate needs.
Establishment of oral rehydration points and distribution of WASH kits.
Recovery Efforts:
Rehabilitation of degraded land and support for agricultural production through improved seeds and tools.
Cash-for-work programs for land and infrastructure repair.
Health and Hygiene:
Cholera prevention and hygiene promotion through trained volunteers and local leaders.
Construction and rehabilitation of water points and emergency latrines.
Community Engagement:
Training community disaster management committees and implementing climate adaptation measures.
Awareness campaigns for hygiene and disease prevention.
Gender and Inclusion:
Ensuring that interventions prioritize women, children, the elderly, and people with disabilities through tailored approaches.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The strategy is necessary because:

Unprecedented Impact: The floods are the worst in decades, with a 737% increase in people affected compared to 2023.
Health Risks: The spread of waterborne diseases like cholera and malaria threatens public health.
Food Insecurity: The destruction of crops and livestock exacerbates food crises in a country where over 3.4 million people already face food insecurity.
Infrastructure Collapse: The damage to roads, latrines, and wells worsens the living conditions of the most vulnerable.
Vulnerability: Niger ranks among the most climate-vulnerable countries globally, making disaster recovery essential.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The strategy prioritizes interventions in the five most affected regions:

Dosso
Maradi
Niamey
Tahoua
Zinder
These areas were selected based on the severity of flood damage and the vulnerability of their populations.</narrative>
  </description>
